============================================================================
DeepONet smoother -- surface (Laplace-Beltrami) multigrid level
============================================================================
SCOPE: the branch consumes a fixed-length residual, so this model is bound
       to ONE DoF count and ONE DoF ordering -- no cross-mesh claim is made.
       Coefficient-space l^2 is NOT L^2(Gamma): no mass matrix is exported.

Level data: level_data/L3
  A           : 1024 x 1024, nnz 9216 (9.00/row)
  symmetry    : max|A - A^T| = 0.000e+00 -> symmetric
  definiteness: Jacobi-scaled spectrum [1.5611e-02, 2.6456e+00], cond 1.69e+02 -> SPD
  coordinates : (1024, 3), |x| in [1.0000, 3.0000]
  kappa       : [1.1000, 2.1000]
  ref. edge   : 0 constrained DoFs (uniform mesh)
  prolongation: (1024, 256)
  MASS MATRIX : not exported -> L^2(Gamma) norms are unavailable; coefficient l^2 is reported instead
  DoFs 1024 -> 32x32 periodic lattice, Nyquist = 16 modes/direction
  trunk features: trig -> dim 4

Hierarchy: 4 levels, DoFs [16, 64, 256, 1024]
  Galerkin check per level: P^T A_l P versus the separately assembled A_{l-1}
    level 1 -> 0 : max|P^T A P - A_H| = 1.411e+00  (relative 1.907e-01)
    level 2 -> 1 : max|P^T A P - A_H| = 3.716e-01  (relative 4.404e-02)
    level 3 -> 2 : max|P^T A P - A_H| = 7.569e-02  (relative 8.326e-03)
  A nonzero difference is expected and is reported rather than hidden: on a
  curved surface the coarse matrix is assembled with the isoparametric
  mapping, and a coarse basis function is not reproduced exactly by the fine
  cells' maps, so the hierarchy is not exactly Galerkin. Consequently the
  coarse-complement loss builds Q from P^T A P (an exact A-orthogonal
  projection), while the two-grid step uses the assembled A_H (what the
  actual multigrid cycle uses).
  coarse operator for the loss = P^T A P, densified for the Cholesky solve: (256, 256) (0.52 MB)
  fine A_h remains sparse throughout

Generating errors. train/val/test use independent seeds and have
DISJOINT discrete signatures, so no base function -- nor a rescaled
copy of one -- can appear in two splits.
